INT. TELEGRAPH OFFICE - DUST CREEK - DAY

A cramped frontier room. Wanted posters, a potbellied stove gone cold, a telegraph key on a scarred desk. Dust in the slanting light.

CALEB MARROW (40s, sheriff, tin star dented, sun-creased eyes) stands at the window with his rifle. At the desk, OPAL TREVINO (20s, telegraph operator, ink on her cuffs) taps the key. In the corner, tied to a chair, sits WILLIS PRUITT (30s, outlaw, grin, split lip).

PRUITT
You can wire all the way to Denver, Sheriff. They won't send help.

CALEB
Opal, keep tapping.

OPAL
I am. Nobody answers.

PRUITT
Told you.

CALEB
You told me nothing, Willis. You talked.

Boots crunch on gravel outside. Many. Pruitt turns his head toward the window and raises his voice.

PRUITT
(calling out)
Alive and patient, boys!

CALEB
Quiet.

Caleb racks the lever on his rifle. Opal's hand flutters at her throat and she turns back to the key.

OPAL
It's not going through. Wire's cut.

CALEB
Then we do it slow.

PRUITT
You could just let me go. Save the trouble.

CALEB
Trouble's already here.

Opal looks up. Caleb meets her eye. A beat of understanding.

CALEB (CONT'D)
Opal. Back door. Take the mule.

OPAL
I'm not leaving you.

CALEB
Ride to Fort Hale. Tell the major.

PRUITT
That's forty miles.

OPAL
Thirty-eight.

PRUITT
It's a poor plan, darlin'.

OPAL
You mind your mouth.

CALEB ^
You mind your mouth.

Silence. Even Pruitt grins.

OPAL
(softly)
We say it together now?

CALEB
Seems we do.

Opal pulls her shawl tight, tucks a pencil behind her ear, and slips through the back door. Hinges squeak. A mule snorts in the yard.

OPAL (O.S.)
Don't you die before I'm back, Caleb Marrow!

Hoofbeats fade.

PRUITT
She'll be dead by supper.

CALEB
Not that one.

He settles the rifle on the sill. Outside, the boots stop.

CALEB (CONT'D)
Willis, you still talking?

PRUITT
Only when it helps.

CALEB
Then tell your friends to come in one at a time.
